Why: the code manager allocates code for dynamic methods with malloc() and 
only mprotect()s it executable on non-Win32 hosts.  Win32 builds of 2007 
ran as 32-bit processes where DEP was opt-in; a 64-bit Windows process 
always has DEP, so the first call into a dynamic method jumps into a 
non-executable heap page.  Seen on MSYS2 UCRT64: the x86_64 mono.exe 
segfaults at a heap address (rip=0x804070) as soon as it runs the 
cscc-built mcs.exe ('mono mcs.exe --version').  On Win32, take the 
VirtualAlloc (mono_valloc, PAGE_EXECUTE_READWRITE) path for dynamic chunks 
too.  New patch (not in Guix).

diff --git a/mono/utils/mono-codeman.c b/mono/utils/mono-codeman.c
index 92cb35a4..b1de710d 100644
--- a/mono/utils/mono-codeman.c
+++ b/mono/utils/mono-codeman.c
@@ -221,7 +221,14 @@ new_codechunk (int dynamic, int size)
 
 	pagesize = mono_pagesize ();
 
+#ifdef PLATFORM_WIN32
+	/* malloc()ed memory is not executable under DEP, which is always on for
+	 * 64 bit processes, and the mprotect below is skipped on Win32: give
+	 * dynamic methods VirtualAlloc()ed RWX chunks as well. */
+	if (0) {
+#else
 	if (dynamic) {
+#endif
 		chunk_size = size;
 		flags = CODE_FLAG_MALLOC;
 	} else {
